      "note": "The root box is right: ṣ-f-r is the ordinary Arabic colour root, with the elative-pattern colour adjective aṣfar / fem. ṣafrāʔ 'yellow' (Wiktionary s.v. صفراء: 'feminine singular of أَصْفَر (ʔaṣfar)', root ص ف ر), and the derivatives ṣufrah 'yellowness', ʿuṣfur 'safflower', iṣfirār 'turning yellow'. The right-hand branch of the chart (ṣafrāʔ 'yellow/bile' → Persian صفرا, Italian saffiore → safflower) is sound. The left branch is not: making زَعْفَران (zaʿfarān) a derivative of ṣ-f-r is not accepted by modern scholarship (see node #1), which is also why Nourai's own three marginal NOTEs contradict each other. Of those NOTEs: PLA:145 (Asbaghi) really does derive zaʿfarān from Persian zar-parān and that view is still current enough to be cited on Wiktionary; the two CEL6 claims are worthless — an Indo-European *kes- 'hair' source is phonetically impossible for a word with ز and ع, and the claim that the Arabs got saffron from Kashmir only in the 9th-10th century is refuted by the word's appearance in pre-Islamic Arabic poetry and by Akkadian azupīru.",
