          "derivation": "Arabic زَعْفَران (zaʿfarān) 'saffron, Crocus sativus' is old and well attested (pre-Islamic poetry; Rāzī's medical manuscripts, cited by Devic), but deriving it from the root ṣ-f-r 'yellow' as this arrow does is rejected by modern scholarship: the initial ز (z) and medial ع (ʿ) cannot be reconciled with ص-ف-ر, and no Arabic grammarian derives it that way. AHD states flatly '[...] from Arabic zaʿfarān; akin to Akkadian azupīru, of unknown origin'; Etymonline likewise 'from Arabic az-zaʿfaran, of unknown origin'. Wiktionary offers three competing accounts — a borrowing from Aramaic ṣpr with an added -ān, an internal Arabic formation on the pattern of زعتر zaʿtar and عصفر ʿuṣfur (this is the only one that touches ṣ-f-r, and only obliquely), and a Middle Iranian borrowing. Nourai's own NOTE reports Asbaghi (PLA:145), which I read on the page: 'zaʿfarān: \"Safran\" pers., wahrscheinlich aus dem pers. زرپران zarparān \"mit goldenen Blättern\", mand. zaparania, syr. ...' — i.e. Asbaghi makes it a Persian loan, the opposite direction from the arrow drawn. Against that, Dehkhoda quotes al-Jawāliqī's al-Muʿarrab p.173, 'عربی صحیح است' ('it is genuine Arabic'). The honest verdict is 'origin uncertain, certainly not a transparent ṣ-f-r derivative'.",
